WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.700
I haven't been saying almost anything about
political endorsements this cycle and people

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:06.700
who have [inaudible]

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:10.190
but watching or listening to the show for
a while probably can guess why, which is that

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:16.099
I do not believe that political endorsements
really matter, at least not the vast majority

00:00:16.099 --> 00:00:21.220
of them and particularly not this far into
a democratic primary where there have been

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.580
so many debates it's been going on for so
long.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:31.070
Most people are so familiar with the candidates,
uh, and even those who are not, are not particularly

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:35.620
going to be making decisions on the basis
of one endorsement or another in a way that

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.210
could move the needle.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:38.940
And I've said before that that's really the
key thing.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.289
The needle doesn't get moved that much by
endorsements.

00:00:42.289 --> 00:00:48.219
Endorsements are the type of thing that endorsements
from media outlets generate more media about

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:52.239
the endorsements, but they don't really move
the needle very much.

00:00:52.239 --> 00:00:56.910
That being said, they can be interesting to
look at because they do tell us some interesting

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:02.579
things about what the establishment might
be thinking or what legacy corporate media

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:03.579
things.

00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:09.450
Or we might look at reactions to endorsements,
which can sometimes tell us something interesting

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:14.390
about a race, and that's the case with the
New York times endorsements that were made

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:19.990
yesterday after a whole bunch of fanfare and
pomp and circumstance and releasing of little

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:24.990
video excerpts of the interviews that the
New York times did with each of the different

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:26.220
democratic candidates.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:32.320
The New York times opinion page has issued
its endorsement and they have broken with

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.350
convention, they've decided to endorse two
candidates.

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:42.280
Now importantly, I saw tweets saying they
endorsed a ticket in endorsing two candidates.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:43.619
They did not.

00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:48.509
The New York times says that they are endorsing
two separate candidates, not a ticket that

00:01:48.509 --> 00:01:54.790
should run together, and those candidates
are Amy Klobuchar and Elizabeth Warren.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:59.869
The New York times points out that they spent
a whole bunch of time with the candidates

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:05.619
that this is a key choice for Democrats who
wanted to feed Trump and so on and so forth.

00:02:05.619 --> 00:02:09.080
Giving context for why this is the way they
decided to do it.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:14.190
And their conclusion is that the most effective
candidates for each of the two approaches

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:19.630
that they say Democrats could choose are Elizabeth
Warren and Amy Klobuchar.

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:24.580
So the two approaches you can probably guess
are a more progressive approach where they're

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:28.980
saying it's Warren, not Bernie, that Democrats
should go with.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:34.830
And a more centrist establishment approach
where they're saying it is Klobuchar not Joe

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:39.870
Biden or Pete Buddha judge that they should
go with.

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:45.940
These are my words in terms of calling them
the progressive and the centrist as I interpret

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:49.140
what the New York times writes, they use other
phrasiology.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:55.500
There's a paragraph also about Bernie Sanders
being 79 at an inauguration time.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:00.290
They mentioned that he had a heart attack
and they also write that Bernie's lack of

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:08.060
compromising and over promising is a reason
why the progressive choice should not be Bernie.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:09.270
It should be Warren.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:14.120
If Democrats say that's the way they want
to go, they say, quote, Mr. Sanders would

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:20.030
be 79 when he assumed office and after an
October heart attack, his health is a serious

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:21.340
concern.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.830
Then there's how Mr. Sanders approaches politics.

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:28.140
He boasts that compromise is anathema to him.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.940
Only his prescriptions can be the right ones.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.340
Even though most are overly rigid, untested,
and divisive.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:38.430
He promises that once in office, a ground
swell of support will emerge to push through

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:39.700
his agenda.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:43.890
Three years into the Trump administration,
we see little advantage to exchanging one

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:48.270
over promising divisive figure in Washington
for another good news.

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:55.560
Then they say that Elizabeth Warren has emerged
as a standard bearer for the democratic left

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:00.250
and the New York times goes on to list a bunch
of different reasons why they say Warren is

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:06.530
the way to go for the progressive choice rather
than Bernie as a they.

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:07.530
They.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:13.950
Those are, first of all, she is a gifted storyteller
in terms of how the system is rigged.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:18.980
Now notice that each, a lot of these pros
are thinly veiled digs at Bernie, so when

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:23.190
they say she's a gifted storyteller about
how the system is rigged, which feels less

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:25.450
bombastic than other ideas.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:26.720
That's a reference to Bernie.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:32.230
Bernie is using other language and is applying
bombast which they are being implicitly critical

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:33.230
of.

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:38.870
They say they like that Elizabeth Warren is
committed to reform rather than tearing down

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:42.110
and rebuilding it, which is more of the burning
approach.

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:48.190
Very early on I did a story about even if
on policy they are similar on many issues.

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:49.870
Bernie and Warren have some real differences.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:54.690
One of those differences is the approach,
the idea of Elizabeth Warren reforming the

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:57.410
things not working about the system.

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:00.800
Bernie tearing down the system and rebuilding
it all together.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.720
When you read between the lines, this bullet
point about how Warren is committed to reform

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:10.290
is a thinly veiled or maybe not so thinly
veiled critique of Bernie Sanders.

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:13.430
They say campaign policy plans are serious.

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:17.920
When it comes to Elizabeth Warren, it seems
to be an implication that maybe they see Bernie's

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:20.820
campaign plans is not so serious.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:26.570
They also say Elizabeth Warren should be the
progressive choice for the democratic party

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:29.900
because she will increase federal investment
in clean energy research.

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:32.000
She'll wean us off of fossil fuels.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:37.270
She will expand universal childcare, free
public college and social security.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:44.860
So then there is why Klobuchar for the moderate
centrists and the New York times points out

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:50.310
that if you want to go with a more establishment
candidate, a more centrist candidate, that

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:54.870
even though Biden is winning the polls, it's
only because of familiarity more than anything

00:05:54.870 --> 00:06:01.550
else and that Biden's age is a sign that he
should be passing the torch and they say that

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:04.950
it is Amy Klobuchar who should have the torch
passed to her.

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:11.650
They say quote good news then that Amy Klobuchar
has emerged as a standard bearer for the democratic

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:12.650
center.

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:17.020
Her vision goes beyond the incremental, given
the polarization in Washington and beyond,

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:21.880
the best chance to enact many progressive
plans could be under a Klobuchar administration.

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:28.570
They go on to explain the Senator from Minnesota
is the very definition of Midwestern charisma,

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:30.860
grit and stick to witness.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:35.240
Her lengthy tenure in the Senate and bipartisan
credentials would make her a deal maker, a

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:42.010
real one, and uniter for the wings of the
party and perhaps the nation, you know, in

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:47.120
looking at the reaction to Amy Klobuchar during
the debates and looking at how the progressive

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:52.210
left reacts to her, I don't know that she
would actually be a uniter of the wings of

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:53.210
the party.

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:56.340
But again, this is the New York times assessment
of this.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:01.419
What they are effectively saying is she's
slightly more progressive than Biden.

00:07:01.419 --> 00:07:06.139
She's younger, and from a practical perspective,
she might get more done.

00:07:06.139 --> 00:07:11.410
So this is the endorsement of the New York
times two options, progressive or centrist,

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:15.280
if you want to go progressive, the New York
times says, take Warren if you want to go

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:16.280
centrist.

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:18.919
The New York times says, take Amy Klobuchar.

00:07:18.919 --> 00:07:20.420
So here's the deal.

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:25.100
I don't think the New York times has any responsibility
to choose people they think will win.

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:27.960
So as an example, Amy Klobuchar has no shot.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:33.910
She's continuing to pull 3% nationally, sees
she's in fifth place, place in Iowa, and likely

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:35.830
will get zero delegates there.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:39.390
She's in fifth place in New Hampshire and
is not going to get any delegates there.

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:42.200
She's in seventh place in Nevada, in South
Carolina.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:45.690
Amy Klobuchar is pulling worse than people
who are no longer even in the race.

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:48.110
Cory Booker in South Carolina, he's out of
the race.

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:52.420
He's pulling better than Amy Klobuchar, so
I don't think that that matters when it comes

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:56.410
to who they endorse, endorse whoever they
think is best based on whatever metrics they're

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:59.100
applying, not who they think they will win.

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:00.680
So they endorsed Klobuchar.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:05.070
If Dems want to go centrist and Warren, if
Dems want to go progressive, the real problem

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:10.260
I have is that they seem to be ignoring the
actual records of candidates in their assessments.

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:16.100
So when they say if the democratic party wants
to go left, go with Warren over Bernie.

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:20.390
They frame Bernie as a simply my way or the
highway type of guy.

00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:25.280
When in reality, Bernie Sanders has had many
successes negotiating with Republicans in

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:26.280
the Senate.

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:31.590
The New York times has even written articles
about Bernie successes negotiating with Republicans

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:32.650
in the Senate.

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:38.090
Amy Klobuchar on the other hand is not really
a more progressive version of Joe Biden in

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:39.440
any real practical sense.

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:44.010
Look at her record voting for Trump's horrible
extremist judges recently.

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:47.720
Uh, which again the New York times has also
covered as has the daily beast.

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:50.710
Uh, and we are showing you an article talking
about that.

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:56.100
So the real issue for me is, you know, endorse
whoever you want that they, it's their endorsement,

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:57.100
right?

00:08:57.100 --> 00:08:58.100
It's not mine.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:03.360
We can disagree about the strategy behind
it, but the problem is that they are using

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:05.570
incorrect assertions.

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:09.440
They are basing their endorsements on facts
that are not really facts.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:15.110
And what I believe that they did is they decided
who they were going to endorse first and they

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:17.060
worked backwards to justify it.

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:22.860
They decided we want to endorse Warren over
Bernie, so let's find a way of justifying

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:23.860
it.

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:25.550
And they said, well, Warren's more pragmatic.

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:28.390
Bernie is a my way or the highway guy, Bernie
will be 79.

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:29.640
When an inauguration comes.

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:35.540
They backed their way in a, it's like a post
facto rationalization to get to where it is.

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:40.190
They decided initially they wanted to get,
let's endorse Klobuchar over Biden.

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:44.570
We can say Biden's age and that is time has
passed, but we'll ignore a whole bunch of

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:48.810
stuff about Klobuchar and we'll be able to
justify the endorsements we want to make anyway,

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:54.150
and if you watch the videos, I know that these
are only excerpts, but go and watch the videos

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:59.290
when they are actually interviewing the different
candidates and look at their demeanor to the

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:01.850
candidates that they ended up not endorsing.

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:07.830
Watch the interview with Bernie Sanders and
honestly tell me that they seem to actually

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:09.950
open to endorsing Bernie Sanders.

00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:11.880
That's not what I saw when I watched it.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:18.660
This is not anything other than saying I believe
that these endorsements were decided upon.

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:23.080
Maybe not explicitly, they might not have
said it, but they knew where they wanted to

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:26.500
go with them and they built a case around
that.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:32.800
More like a defense attorney or a prosecutor,
rather than simply being a juror and saying,

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:35.660
let's look at all the facts and then come
to our conclusion.

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:40.060
In the end, I still don't think this matters,
but I want to hear from you about it.

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:45.210
What do you think, uh, is the goal of the
New York times endorsements, particularly

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:48.110
when Amy Clovis Klobuchar has no chance of
winning?

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:50.840
Uh, do you think it is posturing of some kind?

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:55.380
Do you think it is more meant to just muddle
things up in a, in a way that will end up

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:57.160
being pro establishment?

00:10:57.160 --> 00:10:59.399
Whatever you think about the endorsements,
I want to hear from you.

00:10:59.399 --> 00:11:03.120
If you're watching on YouTube, leave a comment
replying to this video.

